course: dictionary
lesson: 1243
title: One word, many bundles
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bundle is a group of things held together, like sticks tied with string or papers wrapped in one parcel. «A bundle of sticks» means many sticks tied as one. You can often spot the meaning by asking: what is being held together?
explain: Bundle can also be a verb, an action word. To bundle is to tie things together, or to push someone quickly somewhere. «The children bundled into the car» means they were pushed in quickly, not that they tied themselves up.
explain: Sometimes bundle is part of a phrase. «Bundle up» means to dress warmly, but the context matters. «Don't bundle up under blankets to sweat it out» shows it is not always a good idea, especially when you have a fever.
explain: Phrases such as «a bundle of energy» and «a bundle of nerves» use bundle to mean a lot of something packed together. Think of a bundle of energy as someone full of energy, and a bundle of nerves as someone very nervous. Read the whole sentence to work out which meaning fits.
fact: A bundle is a group of things tied or wrapped together.
fact: A bundle can also be a soft parcel, like a baby wrapped in a blanket.
fact: To bundle is to tie things together, or to push someone quickly somewhere.
fact: «Bundle of joy» means a newborn baby.
fact: «Bundle up» means to dress warmly, but a feverish child should not be bundled in blankets, because they can overheat.
exercise: «A bundle of sticks was tied with string.» What is a bundle here? | *A group of things tied together | A kind of hat | A loud noise | A tiny insect
exercise: Dad says, «The new baby is a bundle of joy.» What does he mean? | A big party | *A newborn baby | A box full of toys | A jumping game
exercise: «Don't bundle up under blankets to sweat it out.» What does bundle up mean here? | Tidy the room | *Wrap up warmly | Sing loudly | Sleep upside down
test: «The stem is a bundle of pipes and a pillar.» What does bundle mean here? | A piece of cloth for a baby | A soft parcel | *A group of things tied or joined together | A loud noise
test: Ella says she feels like a bundle of nerves before her test. What does she mean? | She has lost her bag | She is full of sleepy energy | *She feels very nervous | She is tied up with string
exam: «The children bundled into the car.» What does bundled mean here? | Sang a song | Fell fast asleep | *Were pushed quickly | Walked very slowly
